                    "Reference": [
                      "U.S. Department of the Interior, Fish and Wildlife Service. Handbook of Toxicity of Pesticides to Wildlife. Resource Publication 153. Washington, DC: U.S. Government Printing Office, 1984., p. 22"
                    ],
                    "ExtendedReference": [
                      {
                        "Citation": "U.S. Department of the Interior, Fish and Wildlife Service. Handbook of Toxicity of Pesticides to Wildlife. Resource Publication 153. Washington, DC: U.S. Government Printing Office, 1984., p. 22",
                        "Matched": {
                          "PCLID": 900102667,
                          "Citation": "U.S. Department of the Interior, Fish and Wildlife Service. Handbook of Toxicity of Pesticides to Wildlife. Resource Publication 153. Washington, DC: U.S. Government Printing Office, 1984., p. 22"
                        }
                      }
                    ],
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "/MAMMALS and BIRDS/ /In 3 month old male mallards/ LD50 at 95% confidence level was 265 mg/kg  chlormequat chlorine. Signs of intoxication: Ataxia, miosis, falling, sitting, using wings for pedestrian locomotion, tremors, tetanic seizures, immobility, opisthotonos, and frequent spasms. Signs appeared as soon as 5 min and mortalities usually occurred between 15 and 40 min after /oral/ treatment. Remission took up to 3 days.",
                          "Markup": [
                            {
                              "Start": 95,
                              "Length": 11,
                              "URL": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/chlormequat",
                              "Type": "PubChem Internal Link",
                              "Extra": "CID-13837"
                            },
                            {
                              "Start": 107,
                              "Length": 8,
                              "URL": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/element/Chlorine",
                              "Type": "PubChem Internal Link",
                              "Extra": "Element-Chlorine"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "TOCHeading": "ICSC Environmental Data",
                "Description": "Environmental effects of this chemical, provided by the International Labour Organization's (ILO's) International Chemical Safety Cards (ICSC).",
                "URL": "https://www.ilo.org/safework/info/publications/WCMS_113134/lang--en/index.htm",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 22,
                    "Name": "",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "The substance is harmful to aquatic organisms. Avoid release to the environment in circumstances different to normal use."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "TOCHeading": "Environmental Fate/Exposure Summary",
                "Description": "This section summarizes the environmental fate/exposure of this chemical, including the natural and artificial sources of the chemical as a pollutant, its environmental fate, biodegradation, abiotic degradation, bioconcentration, etc.",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 21,
                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Chlormequat chloride's production may result in its release to the environment through various waste streams; its use as a plant growth regulator will result in its direct release to the environment. If released to air, a vapor pressure of 7.50X10-8 mm Hg at 20 °C indicates chlormequat chloride will exist in both the vapor and particulate phases in the atmosphere. Vapor-phase chlormequat chloride will be degraded in the atmosphere by reaction with photochemically-produced hydroxyl radicals; the half-life for this reaction in air is estimated to be 2.2 days. Particulate-phase chlormequat chloride will be removed from the atmosphere by wet or dry deposition. Chlormequat chloride does not contain chromophores that absorb at wavelengths >290 nm and therefore is not expected to be susceptible to direct photolysis by sunlight. If released to soil, chlormequat chloride is expected to have moderate mobility based upon a Koc of 203. However, this compound exists as a quaternary ammonium cation and cations generally adsorb more strongly to soils containing organic carbon and clay than their neutral counterparts. Volatilization from moist soil is not expected because the base exists as a cation and cations do not volatilize. Chlormequat chloride is not expected to volatilize from dry soil surfaces based upon its vapor pressure. In soil, chlormequat chloride is degraded rapidly by microbial activity. Half-lives in 4 soils were reported as averaging 32 days at 10 °C and 1 to 28 days at 22 °C. If released into water, chlormequat chloride is not expected to adsorb to suspended solids and sediment based upon the Koc. However, this compound exists as a quaternary ammonium cation and therefore would be expected to adsorb strongly to sediments and suspended matter, clay minerals, and other materials with negative surface charges. In water, the primary fate processes for the chlormequat chloride cation are expected to be biodegradation and adsorption. Although no biodegradation data were located for the chlormequat chloride cation in water, chlormequat chloride has been reported to be degraded rapidly by microbial activity in soil, suggesting that biodegradation in water could occur. Chlormequat chloride is a quarternary cation at pH values of 5 to 9 and therefore volatilization from water surfaces is not expected to be an important fate process. An estimated BCF of 3.2 suggests the potential for bioconcentration in aquatic organisms is low. Occupational exposure to chlormequat chloride may occur through inhalation of dust and dermal contact with this compound at workplaces where chlormequat chloride is produced or used. (SRC)",
                          "Markup": [
                            {
                              "Start": 0,
                              "Length": 20,
                              "URL": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Chlormequat%20chloride",
